{"name":"Sugar Beet","hex":"#834253","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sugar beet","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of beet whose root contains a high concentration of sucrose.","Darling; sweetheart"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sugar_beet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sugar beet","blocks":[{"text":"A sugar beet is a plant whose root contains a high concentration of sucrose and is grown commercially for sugar production. In plant breeding, it is known as the Altissima cultivar group of the common beet. Together with other beet cultivars, such as beetroot and chard, it belongs to the subspecies Beta vulgaris subsp. vulgaris but is classified as var. saccharifera. Its closest wild relative is the sea beet."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/276_Beta_vulgaris_L.jpg/330px-276_Beta_vulgaris_L.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sugar_beet","wikidata":"Q151964"},{"title":"Sugar","blocks":[{"text":"Sugar is a class of sweet-tasting, soluble carbohydrates, many of which are used in food. Simple sugars, also called monosaccharides, include glucose, fructose and galactose. Compound sugars, also called disaccharides or double sugars, are molecules made of two bonded monosaccharides; common examples are sucrose, lactose and maltose. White sugar is almost pure sucrose. During digestion, compound sugars are hydrolysed into simple sugars."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Sucre_blanc_cassonade_complet_rapadura.jpg/330px-Sucre_blanc_cassonade_complet_rapadura.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sugar","wikidata":"Q11002"},{"title":"Beetroot","blocks":[{"text":"Beetroot or beet is the taproot portion of a Beta vulgaris subsp. vulgaris plant in the Conditiva Group. The plant is a root vegetable also known as the table beet, garden beet, or dinner beet, or else categorized by color as red beet or golden beet. Its leaves constitute a leaf vegetable called beet greens. It is native to the Azores, Western Europe to the Mediterranean and India.\nBeetroot can be eaten raw, roasted, steamed, or boiled. Beetroot can also be canned, either whole or cut up, and often are pickled, spiced, or served in a sweet-and-sour sauce."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Detroitdarkredbeets.png/330px-Detroitdarkredbeets.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beetroot","wikidata":"Q165437"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Inspecting_sugar_beet_plants.jpg/500px-Inspecting_sugar_beet_plants.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Inspecting_sugar_beet_plants.jpg/1280px-Inspecting_sugar_beet_plants.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Inspecting_sugar_beet_plants.jpg/500px-Inspecting_sugar_beet_plants.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A geneticist evaluates sugar beet plants","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AInspecting_sugar_beet_plants.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/French_sugar_beet_mill%2C_1843.jpg/500px-French_sugar_beet_mill%2C_1843.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/French_sugar_beet_mill%2C_1843.jpg/500px-French_sugar_beet_mill%2C_1843.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"French sugar beet mill in operation in the 1840s","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFrench_sugar_beet_mill%2C_1843.jpg"